\section{Reproducible local Einstein--Hilbert derivation in Gaussian-null coordinates}
\label{app:local-eh-derivation}

This appendix derives the selected scalar source response directly from
the Einstein--Hilbert (EH) boundary current. It makes explicit the local
metric calculation, boundary functionals, endpoint contribution, and
clock conversion underlying the canonical discussion in the main text.
The result is a restricted local calculation with a specified action
prescription. The accompanying symbolic implementations reproduce its
identities; their successful checks do not replace the stated geometric
and variational premises.

\subsection{Domain, orientation, and metric variations}

Initially set $c=1$ and write $C=(8\pi G)^{-1}$. On a smooth null patch
$\mathcal N=\{r=0\}$, use Gaussian-null coordinates $(u,r,x^A)$,
$A=1,2$, with
\begin{equation}
 ds^2=-2r\hkap\,du^2+2\,du\,dr
       +2r\beta_A\,du\,dx^A+q_{AB}\,dx^A dx^B .
 \label{eq:local-metric}
\end{equation}
Only the metric and its first derivatives at $r=0$ enter the calculation.
The cut metric $q_{AB}$ is a general positive-definite symmetric matrix;
its $u$, $r$, and transverse first derivatives are retained. The generator
is $\ell=\partial_u$, with $\nabla_\ell\ell=\hkap\ell$ on
$\mathcal N$. The null location, generator, coordinate identifications,
normalization $g_{ur}=1$, and integration domain
$[u_i,u_f]\times S$ are fixed. We orient the boundary current with the
normal covector $-dr$. Reversing orientation reverses the entire
boundary response consistently.

Let $h_{ab}=\delta g_{ab}$ preserve this chart and null location. On
$\mathcal N$, its only nonzero metric components are $h_{AB}=\delta q_{AB}$;
the independent radial derivatives include
$\partial_r h_{uu}=-2\delta\hkap$ and
$\partial_r h_{uA}=\delta\beta_A$.
All first derivatives of $h_{AB}$ are retained. A dot denotes
$\partial_u$, and $Q=q^{-1}$. Define
\begin{align}
 \epsS&=\sqrt{\det q}\,d^2x,&
 \delta\epsS&=\tfrac12\epsS\operatorname{tr}(Qh),\notag\\
 \theta&=\tfrac12\operatorname{tr}(Q\dot q),&
 \delta\theta&=\tfrac12\operatorname{tr}
     (Q\dot h-QhQ\dot q),\notag\\
 \sigma_{AB}&=\tfrac12\dot q_{AB}-\tfrac12\theta q_{AB},&
 \mu&=\hkap+\tfrac12\theta .
 \label{eq:local-cut-data}
\end{align}
The variation commutes with $\partial_u$ in this fixed domain. No
field equation, spherical symmetry, or stationary cut metric is used
to obtain the local current below.

\subsection{Direct current and the selected boundary action}

For the EH action, the un-oriented current component is
\begin{equation}
 v^r=g^{ab}\delta\Gamma^r{}_{ab}
       -g^{rb}\delta\Gamma^a{}_{ab}
 =-2\delta\hkap-\operatorname{tr}(Q\dot h)
       +\tfrac12\operatorname{tr}(QhQ\dot q).
 \label{eq:local-current}
\end{equation}
For example, this follows by substituting the metric and variation jets
into

\begin{equation}
 \delta\Gamma^a{}_{bc}=\frac12\delta g^{ad}
  (\partial_b g_{dc}+\partial_c g_{db}-\partial_d g_{bc})
 +\frac12g^{ad}
  (\partial_b h_{dc}+\partial_c h_{db}-\partial_d h_{bc}),
 \qquad \delta g^{ab}=-g^{ac}h_{cd}g^{db}.
 \label{eq:local-connection-variation}
\end{equation}
An independent implementation evaluates the equivalent covariant
expression $v^r=\nabla_b h^{rb}-\nabla^r(g^{ab}h_{ab})$, with
$h^{ab}=g^{ac}g^{bd}h_{cd}$. Both retain general cut-metric first jets;
the cancellation of the $\beta_A$ and transverse/radial cut jets in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:local-current} is a result of this fixed-generator
projection, not a proof that generator responses vanish for more
general variations.

The oriented contribution is
$\Theta_{\rm EH}|_{\mathcal N}=-(C/2)\int\epsS v^r\,du$.
Using Eq.~\eqref{eq:local-cut-data} gives
\begin{equation}
 \Theta_{\rm EH}|_{\mathcal N}
 =C\int_{\mathcal N}\left[
   -\mu\,\delta\epsS
   +\frac{\epsS}{2}\sigma^{AB}h_{AB}
   +\delta\{\epsS(\hkap+\theta)\}\right]du .
 \label{eq:local-decomposition}
\end{equation}
This agrees with the fixed-generator scalar/shear part of the null
decomposition of Ref.~\cite{HF17}, Eqs.~(4.27) and (5.3)--(5.5) in
the arXiv v2 numbering. That reference separately retains its corner
terms. Equation~\eqref{eq:local-decomposition} was derived here from
the local EH current; those global terms have not been inferred to
vanish.

Specify the local boundary functionals
\begin{align}
 B_0&=C\int_{\mathcal N}\epsS(\hkap+\theta)\,du,&
 B_\mu&=C\int_{\mathcal N}\epsS\mu\,du,\notag\\
 I_{\rm rate}&=I_{\rm EH}-B_0+B_\mu .
 \label{eq:local-rate-action}
\end{align}
The last line selects this part of the action prescription; any
remaining boundaries and matter/corner terms require their own
controlled data. Direct variation, rather than a choice of energy
function alone, now yields
\begin{equation}
 \Theta_{\rm rate}|_{\mathcal N}
 =C\int_{\mathcal N}\epsS\,\delta\mu\,du
       +\Theta_{\rm shear},
 \qquad
 \Theta_{\rm shear}=\frac C2
       \int_{\mathcal N}\epsS\sigma^{AB}h_{AB}\,du .
 \label{eq:local-rate-response}
\end{equation}
The scalar exchange is
$-C\mu\delta\epsS+\delta(C\epsS\mu)=C\epsS\delta\mu$.
Its field-space exterior derivative is the same in either polarization,
$C\delta\epsS\wedge\delta\mu$. Conservative fixed-source problems
may set $\delta\mu=0$; the displayed source response compares nearby
such problems with other response channels controlled.

\subsection{Endpoint area and tangent nonexpansion}

The action shift in Eq.~\eqref{eq:local-rate-action} has a simple exact
endpoint form. Since $\partial_u\epsS=\theta\epsS$ and the cut

domain is fixed,
\begin{align}
 I_{\rm rate}-I_{\rm EH}
 &=B_\mu-B_0=-\frac C2\int_{\mathcal N}\epsS\theta\,du
   =-\frac C2(A_f-A_i),\notag\\
 \delta(I_{\rm rate}-I_{\rm EH})
 &=-\frac C2(\delta A_f-\delta A_i),
 \qquad A(u)=\int_S\epsS(u).
 \label{eq:local-endpoint-shift}
\end{align}
Fixed endpoint coordinates do not by themselves set this variation to
zero. Impose instead the background and tangent restrictions
\begin{equation}
 \theta=0,\qquad\delta\theta=0
 \quad\hbox{throughout }[u_i,u_f].
 \label{eq:local-tangent-restriction}
\end{equation}
Then
$\partial_u\delta\epsS=\delta(\theta\epsS)
=\epsS\delta\theta+\theta\delta\epsS=0$,
so $\delta A_f=\delta A_i$. Each endpoint area may have a nonzero
variation. Thus the variation of this particular endpoint difference
vanishes on the tangent sector, and $\delta\mu=\delta\hkap$ there.
With the shear response separately fixed or accounted for, the scalar
result is
\begin{equation}
 \Theta^{(0)}_{\kappa}
 =C\int_{\mathcal N}\epsS\,\delta\hkap\,du .
 \label{eq:local-geometric-scalar}
\end{equation}
This argument controls the endpoint term actually present in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:local-endpoint-shift}. It does not eliminate unrelated
corner responses. A useful stronger subcase is
$h_{AB}=\dot h_{AB}=0$: Eq.~\eqref{eq:local-current} then gives
Eq.~\eqref{eq:local-geometric-scalar} directly from EH, even before
the boundary-functional exchange.

\subsection{Physical clock, length work, and the complementary corner check}

Restore the EH action normalization $c^3/(16\pi G)$ and choose the
fixed physical-clock dictionary
$du=c\,d\tau$, $\hkap=\kappa/c^2$, with $c$ and $G$ fixed.
Both factors are needed:
\begin{equation}
 \frac{c^3}{8\pi G}\int\epsS\,
       \delta(\kappa/c^2)\,c\,d\tau
 =\frac{c^2}{8\pi G}\int d\tau\int_S\epsS\,\delta\kappa .
 \label{eq:local-si-clock}
\end{equation}
The endpoint shift in physical action units is
$-c^3(A_f-A_i)/(16\pi G)$. For uniform stationary data on a fixed
duration $\Delta\tau$, take the positive branch $\kappa>0$ and
$L_\kappa=c^2/\kappa>0$. It
gives
\begin{equation}
 \frac{\Theta^{(0)}_\kappa}{\Delta\tau}
 =\frac{Ac^2}{8\pi G}\delta\kappa
 =-\frac{Ac^4}{8\pi G L_\kappa^2}\delta L_\kappa
 =-F_L\delta L_\kappa,
 \qquad P_L=\frac{F_L}{A}=\frac{\kappa^2}{8\pi G}.
 \label{eq:local-length-pullback}
\end{equation}
This is the selected source one-form. The complete independent-coordinate
